== Effect size semantics ==
Cohen's ''d'' standardizes a difference in means by a standard-deviation estimate under a particular design. It does '''not''' mean that self-created products command a 57% price premium, nor that 57% of subjects are affected. Interpreting a pooled effect requires the original studies' outcome scales and design comparability.

== Alternative causal explanations ==
Candidate variables include competence enhancement, effort justification, psychological ownership, belief of authorship, identity investment and successful completion. These overlap but are not equivalent. Studies separating perceived ownership from authorship or completion help discriminate explanations; none licenses a claim of a uniquely identified universal mechanism.

== Decision-audit protocol (proposed, not validated) ==
For an evaluated artifact define independent axes: (A) creator attachment, (B) measured fit to purpose, and (C) third-party valuation. Record goals before observing outcomes. Obtain blinded or independent ratings where practical, compare baselines and alternatives using the same tests, and evaluate the marginal future consequences of additional investment. The protocol is an engineering synthesis, not an IKEA-effect measurement instrument or clinically proven intervention.

For software/agent systems, outcome variables might include passing tests, deployment reliability, maintainability, user task completion and cost. Transferring consumer-construction findings to AI-assisted or collective authoring remains a hypothesis until tested in those settings.

== Epistemic boundaries ==
Personal valuation is not itself a deviation from rationality; error is defined relative to a specific decision objective. Distinguish direct findings from theoretical accounts, proposal-level safeguards and open research questions. See [[IKEA effect]], [[Cognitive bias]], [[Sunk cost effect]] and [[Validation]].

